{"conjecture": "Synthesis from input-output examples fails to produce commitment-preserving code unless the SMT solver is constrained by an explicit logical specification of the program's semantic invariants, not merely positive examples. The decisive technique is counterexample-guided inductive synthesis (CEGIS) where the verifier checks candidate programs against a background theory that axiomatizes the abstract specification's preconditions and postconditions, using SMT to synthesize not just the executable body but also a proof certificate that the body entails the postcondition under the precondition. Programs synthesized this way hold their commitments because the SMT solver treats the abstract specification as a hard logical constraint during synthesis, not a post-hoc validation target."}
